# HOST suites — assert the LIVE deployed layer; run post-deploy by deploy-live.sh, NEVER in the
# tree verdict (the deployed-layer bootstrap circle).
#
# FORMAT (frozen contract — LAND_PIPELINE_V2 §4.2.2): plain text, one `tests/<name>.bats` per line,
# `#` comments and blank lines ignored. Consumers:
#   scripts/postland-verify.sh  runs `tests/*.bats` MINUS these lines  ⇒ the TREE verdict
#   scripts/deploy-live.sh      runs exactly these, AFTER an advance   ⇒ the LIVE HOST verdict
# The partition is a SET DIFFERENCE, so it is total by construction and never hand-synced. A missing
# manifest means EMPTY: the verifier then runs everything (fail-closed toward MORE proof, never
# less). An unparseable line is ignored and logged rather than silently narrowing the corpus.
#
# WHY this file exists: these suites assert the state of ~/.claude — the DEPLOYED layer. The tree
# under verification is by definition ahead of what is deployed, so pre-deploy they can only fail;
# but the deploy waits on the verdict they are failing. That circle is why 22 postland stamps
# produced 0 greens and every deploy since has been manual. Post-deploy, against the layer they
# actually describe, they are exactly the right check — so they move, they do not get deleted.
#
# ADDING one: a suite that reds in the verifier and passes standalone against the live layer is a
# host suite. Normal attribution already names the file, so it moves here by a one-line land, and
# the next tree carries the new partition with it.
#
# BEFORE YOU ADD ONE — THE UNIT IS THE FILE, SO CHECK THE WHOLE FILE (2026-07-31). Listing a suite
# does not exempt the live-layer TESTS in it; it exempts every test in it, from the tree verdict AND
# from the land smoke (ship-land.sh filter_host_suites) alike. So a file that mixes hermetic tests
# with live-layer ones rides ALL of them out of the gate the moment one of them needs to be here,
# and nothing names the loss — the entry looks exactly like a correct one.
# That was live in tests/deploy-parity.bats: 31 tests, 29 fully hermetic (fixture repo + bindir +
# live root under $BATS_TEST_TMPDIR), 2 reading the real ~/bin and ~/.claude. Measured at 09a0214a,
# a land touching scripts/deploy-parity-assert.sh selected exactly one direct suite — that file —
# and the filter removed it, so the land ran ZERO tests; the exit-3 third-state regression guards
# (7a40d5a8, "could not compare" is not parity) were first exercised only AFTER the change was live.
# Fixed by SPLITTING, not by loosening this contract: the live pair moved to
# tests/deploy-parity-live.bats (listed below) and the hermetic 29 went back into the gate.
# The provenance confirms the split lands on exactly the right seam: at 9609fca1 — the land that
# SEEDED this manifest — `@test` #8 of tests/deploy-parity.bats was "the real repo passes its own
# assertion (guards the live host deployment)", and "deploy-parity test 8 is TRUE-red" is the
# reason recorded for admitting the file (docs/plans/LAND_PIPELINE_V2.md, deploy amend log
# 2026-07-28: the live layer lacked the skills/video-understanding symlink). That test is one of
# the two now in tests/deploy-parity-live.bats. So the file's entire justification for being here
# moved with it, and the 29 that never had one stopped paying for it.
# So: admit a file here only when its live-layer dependence is the WHOLE file. If it is a minority
# of the tests, split the file first — the per-file granularity is the contract, and matching the
# file boundary to the partition boundary is how you honour it rather than amend it.
# tests/deploy-parity.bats now asserts BOTH directions of that split against this manifest, so
# re-listing it (or deleting the live half) is a named RED rather than a silent regression.
#
# DE-LISTED 2026-08-08 — FIVE ENTRIES THAT WERE NEVER HOST SUITES (backlog 22b839c85a52). The
# section above says admit a file only when its live-layer dependence is the WHOLE file. Five of the
# six non-wrapper entries had NONE of it: desk-arm-live (8 tests), desk-recycle-durable (9),
# lr-team-audit (3), session-continue (23), waiting-recycle (108). 151 tests were excluded from the
# tree verdict AND from the land smoke for a property not one of them has. They are gone from the
# list below; only tests/deploy-parity-live.bats, whose two tests resolve the real ~/bin and
# ~/.claude, remains.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT, and it is the ONE that can distinguish the two cases. Green in a fresh cell
# proves nothing here — the bootstrap circle is precisely that a live-layer suite passes while the
# live layer happens to agree with the tree. So each suite was run in a fresh detached worktree with
# `HOME` pointed at an EMPTY directory: ~/.claude does not exist. A suite whose subject is the
# deployed layer CANNOT pass that. All five did, at full count (8/8, 9/9, 3/3, 23/23, 108/108), and
# identically under the real $HOME. CONTROL, run through the same harness on the same box:
# tests/deploy-parity-live.bats goes RED on `not ok 1 the real repo passes its own assertion (guards
# the live host deployment)`. The probe can see the property; the five greens are not vacuous.
#
# THIS REPO HAD ALREADY MEASURED IT ONCE, which is why the de-listing is a closure and not a new
# claim. docs/research/POSTLAND-CELL-BISECT-2026-07-29.md ran "the 6 convicted suites" in a fresh
# cell three times — C1 (session PATH), C2 (v1 daemon PATH), D1 (at the convicting sha 59f7eb38) —
# and got 155 ok / 0 not-ok every time. The one not-ok that ever appeared (D2, reused cell) was
# isolated by a same-minute control to deploy-parity ALONE. So the tree verdict was never red
# because of these five; it was red because of the one file whose live half now lives in
# tests/deploy-parity-live.bats. The count in docs/plans/LAND_PIPELINE_V2.md §"failing = exactly the
# 6 host suites" is the pre-bisect reading, annotated there rather than rewritten.
#
# WHAT THE DE-LISTING COST, because it was not free and the bill is the interesting part. These
# suites did not assert the live layer, but four of them READ ambient inputs, and this list was the
# only thing keeping that off the critical path of a verdict that AUTO-REVERTS. All were named by
# the tree's own scripts/test-hermeticity-lint.sh once its grandfather lists were emptied, and all
# were pinned in the same land, which is what earned the de-listing:
#   · $HOME unfixtured (rule 1) — desk-arm-live, lr-team-audit, session-continue, waiting-recycle.
#   · CC_TELEMETRY_DIR unpinned (rule 5) — desk-arm-live, desk-recycle-durable, session-continue.
#     Its default /tmp/cc-telemetry is ABSOLUTE, so a fixtured $HOME does not redirect it: these
#     three were reading the operator's LIVE session telemetry.
#   · CC_FIRE_CAPACITY_GATE unpinned (rule 2) — waiting-recycle. Inert today (every fire is stubbed
#     through CC_WR_HANDOFF_FIRE), pinned anyway: the ratchet's job is the gap between inert and
#     cannot.
# The eight matching grandfather lines are DELETED in the same diff — the lint reds if you pin a
# seam and leave its line, which is the property that keeps a ratchet from becoming an exemption
# list.
#
# ONE FINDING WORTH MORE THAN THE DE-LISTING. Under the empty-$HOME probe, session-continue.bats
# deposited .claude/autonomy/idl.jsonl and .claude/logs/session-continue.log — it had been appending
# to the operator's live audit trail on every run. Its setup() has isolated the IDL since the
# 404-leaked-lines fix of 2026-07-25… by exporting CC_IDL, which hooks/session-continue.sh does not
# read. That hook reads CONTINUE_IDL / CONTINUE_LOG (hooks/session-continue.sh:58-59); CC_IDL is a
# real seam belonging to hooks/lib/idl-log.sh and boundary-handoff.sh, so the export was neither
# wrong nor doing what its comment claimed. A seam bound under a name the subject does not read
# looks exactly like a closed leak, and nothing in the tree could tell the difference — the leak was
# found by giving the suite an ABSENT $HOME and reading what appeared in it, not by inspection.
#
# SECOND admission criterion — WHOLE-TREE META-LINT WRAPPERS (tests/test-hermeticity-lint.bats and
# tests/test-walltime-lint.bats). These two are here for a different reason than the one above: not
# because they assert the live layer, but because their whole-tree scans are LOAD-SENSITIVE, and a
# full corpus is the load. Clean-room corpora failed on exactly the hermeticity wrapper and nothing
# else (2,085 ok / 1 not-ok and 2,242 ok / 1 not-ok; backlogs b59eb997d035, b4e49b4b5014), which
# localises the 14-consecutive-red-stamp deadlock to one file. The walltime wrapper is admitted on
# the CLASS, not on its own mid-corpus proof: it carries the same shape (`@test "the real tree is
# CLEAN — the embedded ratchet matches HEAD"`), so it inherits the same risk, and it is redundant
# DETECTION — the check it wraps is already enforced standalone.
#
# CAUSE — CORRECTED 2026-07-29 (b4e49b4b5014, the reproduction). The original entry here said "a
# bats suite that wraps a WHOLE-TREE assertion cannot run mid-corpus … it sees a tree the corpus is
# concurrently using". That is WRONG, and it is worth correcting because it is the rationale a
# future reader will use to decide whether these two may come back. The corpus never mutates the
# tree (watched for a full run: 149 and 155 *.bats, zero changes). What actually happened: the lint's
# two pure predicates ran a bare `grep -q`, so rc=2 — grep COULD NOT RUN, fork exhaustion at the
# measured load 15-48 — was indistinguishable from rc=1, "no match". A single transient fork failure
# therefore FABRICATED a LEAK, or "the embedded allowlist is stale", about a perfectly clean tree.
# The corpus's only role was to be the load. That, and only that, explains all three observations
# the "cannot run mid-corpus" story cannot: ~1 not-ok in 2,242 rather than the 4 a deterministic
# whole-tree defect would give; WHICH of the suite's four whole-tree scans fails varying by run
# (`the real tree is CLEAN` in b59eb997d035, the SYMLINK selftest in b4e49b4b5014); and the recorded
# outcome `pass-on-retry` at loadavg 17.38 — a deterministic assertion cannot pass on retry.
# Fixed at the source: afaf40de (rc>=2 is a NON-VERDICT, never a leak) + ed4e6c6a (retry the pure
# predicates 3x before condemning a run). RED-proved by replaying the pre-fix artifact: at afaf40de~1
# under an intermittent grep the whole-tree run exits 1 naming three clean suites as LEAKs and
# --selftest reports "the embedded allowlist is stale"; at HEAD both are green.
#
# WHY THEY NEVERTHELESS STAY HERE: the premise above is fixed, not the appetite. Re-admitting them
# puts a load-sensitive whole-tree scan back on the critical path of a verdict that AUTO-REVERTS, for
# detection that is already enforced standalone — no upside, non-zero downside. Re-admission is a
# deliberate call, not a cleanup; do not treat this correction as license for it.
#
# ENFORCEMENT — what these moves DO cost, recorded honestly. The whole-tree scan is fully preserved:
# postland-verify runs both scripts STANDALONE and whole-tree-strict BEFORE the corpus, so a real
# violation is a deterministic named RED that outranks a cut. The `--selftest` DISCRIMINATION proof
# is NOT: the prelint invokes `./scripts/<lint>.sh tests`, never `--selftest`, so the 17 cases that
# prove the ratchet actually goes RED on a new leak and on a stuck entry run only in these wrappers
# and in scripts/nightly-regression.sh — whose launchd job com.claude.nightly-regression is staged
# in ~/Library/LaunchAgents but NOT loaded (`launchctl print` → "Could not find service"). So today
# that proof runs nowhere automatically. Tracked as backlog 76644e76aaae, not silently accepted.
# Partly mitigated by being HERE rather than deleted: deploy-live.sh runs exactly these lines AFTER
# an advance, so post-deploy — against the live layer, the one place a whole-tree assertion has a
# stable subject — both wrappers run in full, `--selftest` included. That is a lagging check, not a
# gate, which is why the gap is filed rather than closed. It is also how the symlink-selftest defect
# was originally caught (f37a84cf: ROOT derived from a bare `dirname "$0"` resolved to ~/.claude,
# which has no tests/) — a real finding, and a DIFFERENT defect from the load-sensitivity above.
#
# The distinction matters when reading a red: a violation reported by the pre-corpus lint is about
# THE TREE; the same violation reported by one of these wrappers mid-corpus was about the LOAD.

tests/deploy-parity-live.bats

# whole-tree meta-lint wrappers (see the SECOND admission criterion above) — the checks they wrap
# are enforced standalone and pre-corpus by postland-verify.sh; re-run here post-deploy against the
# live layer, which is the only place a whole-tree assertion has a stable subject.
tests/test-hermeticity-lint.bats
tests/test-walltime-lint.bats
